Initial release — offline-first sync for a loan book

The engine a field officer's handset syncs against, built for a trading
centre with no signal rather than for a datacentre.

Three decisions carry the design. Ids are minted by the CLIENT out of a shard
it has claimed, so a phone with no signal can create a row that will never
collide with one the server made — the id space is partitioned three ways and
cannot overlap: shard 0 is legacy and the Firestore import, 1..999,998 are
handsets, and 999,999 is the server.

`rev` is a counter, never a timestamp, and it is handed out under a lock held
for the whole write transaction. Without that lock a writer can take rev 5
while another takes rev 6 and commits first; a device then stores cursor 6 and
rev 5 is not late, it is gone from that device forever. The lock is spelled
three ways across the four drivers the kernel supports, and SQL Server's needs
HOLDLOCK or it ends with the statement instead of the transaction.

A pull page is a prefix of the GLOBAL rev order, because one scalar cursor can
only be honest if everything at or below it was delivered across every entity.

The entity catalogue is an ALLOWLIST generated from the migrated schema: a
sync endpoint takes an entity name and a bag of fields from a handset, so
without a fixed list "update customer 7" and "update customer 7, and move them
to a branch you do not work at" are the same request.
